s with comprehensive ground-truth annotations and traceable manipulation histories. Preliminary evaluations using state-of-the-art forensic systems reveal substantial vulnerabilities in both detection and localization, underscoring the need for unified forensic approaches that remain robust to the rapidly evolving capabilities of modern generative and image editing models.\n\n## Metrics\n\n1 Record Views\n\n## Details\n\nTitle\n    \n\nThe Safe Image Authenticity Challenge: Detecting and Localizing Partial and Fully Synthetic Manipulations\n\nCreators\n    \n\nTai Duc Nguyen - Drexel University\n\nMatthew C Stamm - Drexel University, Electrical and Computer Engineering\n\nJill Crisman - UL Research Institutes\n\nJesse Hostetler - UL Research Institutes\n\nLaura Cassano - Aptima (United States)\n\nMichael Davinroy - Aptima (United States)\n\nPeter Bautista - Aptima (United States)\n\nPublication Details\n    \n\n2026 IEEE/CVF Winter Conference on Applications of Computer Vision Workshops (WACVW)\n\nConference\n    \n\n2026 IEEE/CVF Winter Conference on Applications of Computer Vision Workshops (WACVW) (Tucson, Arizona, United States, 06 Mar 2026–10 Mar 2026)\n\nPublisher\n    \n\nIEEE\n\nResource Type\n    \n\nConference paper\n\nLanguage\n    \n\nEnglish\n\nAcademic Unit\n    \n\nElectrical and Computer Engineering\n\n991022199901104721\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocalizing Perceptual Artifacts in Synthetic Images for Image Quality Assessment via Deep-Learning-Based Anomaly Detection (https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/15/5/916)\nciteturn8search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Localizing Perceptual Artifacts in Synthetic Images for Image Quality Assessment via Deep-Learning-Based Anomaly Detection ... Image Zijin Yin ... To automate the quality assurance process, the research community has recently formulated the task of Perceptual Artifacts Localization (PAL) [7,8], which aims to segment defective regions within generated images pixel-by-pixel, as shown in Figure 1. ... Second, the generalization gap: the visual characteristics of artifacts evolve rapidly with model updates (e.g., artifacts from a GAN differ significantly from those of a diffusion model). ... Rather than training a classifier to explicitly recognize artifacts, we formulate artifact localization as an out-of-distribution detection problem within the pre-trained semantic space.\n\n# Localizing Perceptual Artifacts in Synthetic Images for Image Quality Assessment via Deep-Learning-Based Anomaly Detection\n\nby\n\nZijin Yin\n\nImageZijin Yin\n\nSciProfiles Scilit Preprints.org Google Scholar\n\n^{}^{}\n\nSchool of Artificial Intelligence, Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications, Beijing 100876, China\n\nElectronics 2026, 15(5), 916; https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15050916\n\nSubmission received: 30 January 2026 / Revised: 14 February 2026 / Accepted: 22 February 2026 / Published: 24 February 2026\n\n(This article belongs to the Special Issue Computer Vision and AI Algorithms for Diverse Scenarios)\n\nDownload keyboard_arrow_down\n\nDownload PDF\nDownload PDF with Cover\nDownload XML\nDownload Epub\n\nBrowse Figures\n\nVersions Notes\n\n## Abstract\n\nWhile deep generative models, such as text-to-image diffusion, demonstrate strong capabilities in synthesizing photorealistic images, they frequently produce perceptual artifacts (e.g., distorted structures or unnatural textures) that require manual correction. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - FujitsuResearch/training-free-detection-of-text-to-video-generations-via-over-coherence: This is the official implementation of WACV'26 \"Training-free Detection of Text-to-video Generations via Over-coherence\". TBD 10.2.2025 · GitHub (https://github.com/FujitsuResearch/training-free-detection-of-text-to-video-generations-via-over-coherence)\nciteturn8search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; FujitsuResearch / training-free-detection-of-text-to-video-generations-via-over-coherence Public ... This repository contains the official implementation of our WACV'26 paper: \"Training-free Detection of Text-to-video Generations via Over-coherence.\"\nsubdiag_criteria.py  | subdiag_criteria.py  |    |  \n\n\n# Official WACV'26 Implementation\n\nThis repository contains the official implementation of our WACV'26 paper: \"Training-free Detection of Text-to-video Generations via Over-coherence.\"\n\n## Setup\n\nCreate a Python environment and install dependencies:\n    \n    `python3 -m venv venv\n    ./venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt\n    `\n\n## Prepare data\n\nOur evaluation protocol requires a CSV with the following columns:\n\n  * `video_path`: absolute or relative path to each video file\n  * `benchmark`: data source name (e.g., `ng`, `vf`)\n  * `label`: `1` for generated, `0` for real\n  * `fps`: original frame rate of the video\n  * `model`: generator/model name (in case of generated video)\n\n## Data preparation example: Downloading Generated Videos from Civitai\n\n### `benchmark_from_links.py`\n\nThis script downloads videos and constructs benchmark CSV files used in our experiments. It supports two benchmarks:\n\n  * `ng` — Civitai-generated videos (Negative Generation benchmark)\n  * Saves them to: benchmark_data/vf_videos/{model}_{id}.mp4\n  * Writes `benchmark_data/vf_benchmark.csv` containing:\n  * `video_path` — local path to the downloaded MP4\n  * `benchmark` — `\"vf\"`\n  * `label` — `1`\n  * `model`\n  * `fps`\n\nAfter running the two downloads, the csvs of both real (vf) and generated (ng) data can be joined with\n    \n    `python -c \"import pandas as p; p.concat([p.read_csv('benchmark_data/ng_benchmark.csv'), p.read_csv('benchmark_data/vf_benchmark.csv')], ignore_index=True).to_csv('benchmark_data/ng_vf_benchmark.csv', index=False)\"\n    `\n\n## How to run\n\nFrom the repository root, run:\n\n### Civitai\n    \n    python benchmark_from_links.py ng\n\n### VideoFeedback\n    \n    python benchmark_from_links.py vf\n\nThe script is resumable: already-downloaded videos will be skipped on subsequent runs.\n\n## Run\n\nThe command below evaluates our proposed criteria on the created benchmark. Note it first down-samples 8 fps (5% tolerance) to avoid fps-related biased results.\n    \n    `python run_criteria_all.py   --benchmark benchmark_data/ng_vf_benchmark.csv   --outdir results   --outfile our_criterion_ng_vf_results.csv   --required-fps 8   --fps-ratio-tol 0.05   --perturb-ms 0   --max-frames 16 --compute-clip\n    `\n\nOutputs are written to:\n    \n    `results/our_criterion_results.csv\n    `\n\n## Visualize with boxplots\n\nPlot from the results CSV (`results/our_criterion_results.csv`). Groups are inferred from your benchmark (model names + a \"VF Real\" group).\n    \n    `python plot_results_boxplot.py --csv results/our_criterion_results_ng_vf.csv --out results/boxplot.png\n    `\n\nThe figure below is the output you would get after running the boxplot visualization command on the evaluation results.\n\n## About\n\nThis is the official implementation of WACV'26 \"Training-free Detection of Text-to-video Generations via Over-coherence\". TBD 10.2.2025\n\n### Resources\n\nReadme\n\nActivity\n\nCustom properties\n\n### Stars\n\n1 star\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n0 forks--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCVWW 2026 — Proceedings and Contributed Papers (https://cmp.felk.cvut.cz/cvww2026/proceedings.html)\nciteturn8search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Dynamic Ensemble of Deepfake Detectors Conditioned on CLIP Features\n\n# Published Papers & Proceedings\n\nContributed papers and proceedings for CVWW 2026.\n\nProceedings in PDF\n\n## Contributed Papers\n\n  * Instantaneous Monocular Camera Tilt Stabilization for Autonomous Student Formula\nErik Doležal, Jan Čech\nPaper\n\n  * Pi-GS: Sparse